{{- if eq .Role "epic-planner"}}

EPIC-WRITING SKILL:
Invoke skill: epic-writing when writing each epic for this task.

Key principles from the skill:
- An epic bounds one cohesive capability area, expected to decompose into 3–8 user stories
- Each capability section is locally actionable from its assigned content and
  declared direct references; do not copy inherited goal requirements or rationale
- The Story Writer uses the assigned capability carrier plus its declared direct
  references, without recursively reading sibling or ancestor sections
- Completion criteria must be falsifiable: all story ACs passing satisfies them
- Personas must include environment/skill level where it affects product behavior
- Out of Scope is not optional — it protects Story Writers from scope absorption
- Assumptions resolve behavioral ambiguity only — no premature solutioning
- If a single epic spans multiple independent capability areas, it is too broad — flag for splitting
- Producing multiple well-sized epics from one vision source is normal planner behavior

Anti-patterns to avoid:
- Vision Laundering: capabilities named after technical layers instead of user-facing behavior
- Giant Epic: multiple independent persona clusters or unrelated subsystems in one epic
- Dwarf Epic: single user action with one AC — this is a story, not an epic
- Storified Epic: capabilities written at story granularity (Given/When/Then)
- Unfalsifiable Completion Criteria: "users have a better experience" — not falsifiable
- Scope Absorption: vision says X, you write capabilities for X, Y, and Z
- Opaque Capability: assigned content and declared references omit information the Story Writer needs
{{- else if eq .Role "us-writer"}}

USER-STORY-WRITING SKILL:
Invoke skill: user-story-writing before writing any stories.

Key principles from the skill:
- SMARC criteria: Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, Context-bound
- Every story uses canonical form: "As a <persona>, I want to <action>, so that <outcome>"
- Personas must be specific (environment, skill level) — not generic "User"
- Acceptance criteria are contracts — they define done
- Acceptance criteria must be user-observable
- Assumptions are first-class outputs that resolve behavioral ambiguity
- Edge cases use AC-NNN-Nb suffix
- Self-review checklist must pass before submission

Anti-patterns to avoid:
- Persona Laundering: persona is a fig leaf on a technical task
- Giant Story: cannot be implemented in a single focused session
- Wishful Story: no testable ACs
- Hidden Coupling: references other components without declaring dependency
- Assumption Burial: assumptions embedded in stories instead of surfaced
- Scope Absorption: writing stories beyond what the task scopes
- Premature Solutioning: ACs prescribe implementation instead of behavior
- Generic Persona: "As a User" where "User" could be any noun
- Valueless Story: hand-waving the "so that" clause

